WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:03.920
Before the 16th century, clocks were horrible.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.340
Typically there was one large mechanical clock
in the center of any town which would be reset

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.049
daily, but by the end of the day these would
be off by as much as 20 minutes.

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:17.010
Time was but a rough estimate, but nobody
really had a problem with this since there

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.630
was no expectation of accuracy.

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:23.840
At the time, there was no real need for accurate
clocks in daily life.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.769
The need for more accurate clocks came about
when explorers started to voyage across the

00:00:27.769 --> 00:00:29.619
world by sea.

00:00:29.619 --> 00:00:33.829
The best way for ships to calculate their
locations was to have two clocks—one with

00:00:33.829 --> 00:00:38.760
the time of the ships origin and one with
the current time as calculated by a sundial.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.399
With every four minutes of difference between
the two times, you would know that you had

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:46.629
travelled about 68 miles (one degree of longitude).

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:50.789
The problem with this method was with the
accuracy of the clocks.

00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:54.810
Much like the clocks in town centers, the
origin clocks were off by up to 20 minutes

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:59.719
by the end of one day, so this method was
useless after a few days of travel.

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:04.750
This stifled exploration, so governments around
the world offered prizes equivalent to millions

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.900
of dollars to anyone who could design an accurate
clock.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.110
That’s where Galileo comes into play.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:16.130
Legend has it that in 1583, a 19 year old
Galileo was bored while attending prayers

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:20.390
at the Duomo of Pisa, so he started watching
the hanging lamps up above.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:25.170
He noticed that, as they swung back and forth,
the lamps always took the exact same amount

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.640
of time to complete one swing.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:32.920
No matter if they swung one foot or one inch,
the swing would take the exact same amount

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:33.920
of time.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.740
58 years later, this property was used to
make a pendulum clock.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:43.150
Properly weighted, a pendulum could be designed
to swing once per second, and the clocks would

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.020
use the swinging weight to keep time.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:50.430
This clock was amazingly accurate—a week
after being set, it would only be off by as

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:52.200
much as a minute.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:57.100
100 years after it’s invention, the pendulum
clock was in every house.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:01.590
Many argue that, without this invention, the
industrial revolution would not have been

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:02.590
possible.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:06.060
You could now tell someone to meet you at
an exact time and they would actually be there

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.820
at that time.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:12.010
Ships could now use these accurate clocks
for accurate navigation, and this meant that

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:17.550
it was no longer risky to send ships regularly
to buy and sell goods across the world.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.540
Accurate time also changed the way people
worked.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:25.730
Before the pendulum clock, people only worked
for results—by the end of the day they were

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:29.380
paid for how much they made instead of how
much they worked.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:34.270
With the clock workers worked for an amount
of time, and they were paid by the hour.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:39.560
With the new production boom, manufacturers
demanded a method of fast and cheap shipping,

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.910
and so the railroad was born, but this introduced
a new problem.

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:46.720
Time was still dictated by the sun.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.702
Each town would calculate the local time based
upon the location of the sun in the sky, but

00:02:51.702 --> 00:02:55.870
this meant that from one town to the next,
the time would be different.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:00.560
In one town it could be 8:10 and in the next
town over it would be 8:15.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.890
Both times were correct for the two locations,
so this didn’t really cause a problem since

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:08.150
it would take hours to get to that next town
over.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:12.250
Across the United States, there were thousands
of different local times.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:16.610
However, with the newly invented railroad,
people could get from one town to the next

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:22.030
quickly, but nobody knew when trains really
left since everyone used a different time.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:26.540
The railroad might use one time, while the
origin town would use another, and the destination

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.760
town would have a completely different one.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:33.650
Britain solved this problem by creating a
standardized time—Greenwich Mean Time—and

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:36.890
transmitting that time across the country
by telegraph.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:42.010
The US decided that, with it’s huge size,
a standard time zone would not work, so four

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.810
different time zones were mapped out.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:49.810
Much like Britain, the standard time would
be transmitted across the country by telegraph.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:53.710
The implementation of this new time zone,
however, created one of the stranger days

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:55.540
in human history.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:59.849
The new eastern time zone ran four minutes
behind the time that New York had been using,

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:06.030
so on Sunday, November 18, 1883, the clocks
struck noon, then four minutes later, the

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:10.230
clocks struck noon again as the new time standard
was implemented.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:15.319
The next great innovation in timekeeping came
towards the beginning of the 20th century.

00:04:15.319 --> 00:04:19.840
Pierre and Jacques Currie, two French physicists,
noticed that when electricity was applied

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:23.870
to quartz, it vibrates at a incredibly stable
rate.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:30.030
Knowing that quartz vibrates 32,768 times
a second, clockmakers used this crystal to

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:33.150
make incredibly accurate clocks.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:37.840
These quartz clocks were able to go a month
only loosing or gaining 15 seconds.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.380
Just as the pendulum clock allowed for the
industrial revolution, the quartz clock allowed

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:45.210
for the technological revolution.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:49.210
Without quartz clocks, modern computers would
have been impossible.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:54.490
An accurate internal clock is needed to coordinate
all the different microprocessors.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:58.990
With these new accurate clocks however, people
also began to notice that the earth’s rotation

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:00.810
wasn’t all that regular.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:04.250
It deviated by a few seconds here and there.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:09.940
In the 1950’s the first super-accurate atomic
clocks were built—measuring time based upon

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:14.250
the decay of atomic elements, and this allowed
an International Conference of Weights and

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:18.590
Measures to change what time was in 1967.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:24.220
They decided that a day was no longer one
rotation of the earth—a day was now 86,400

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:26.420
atomic seconds.

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:32.340
Since the time it takes for the earth to rotate
is not exactly 86,400 atomic seconds, we make

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:36.750
up for this by having leap days and leap seconds
every once in a while.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:41.550
The atomic-clock also allowed for nano-second
accuracy, and this is actually pretty important

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:45.750
for many modern technologies—most notably
the GPS.

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:52.220
GPS works by asking three different satellites
the time based upon their onboard atomic clocks.

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:56.720
When the answer comes back, each time will
be slightly different on a nano-second level

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.881
due to satellites being at different distances
and the request for the time going out at

00:06:00.881 --> 00:06:05.380
only the speed of light, so the GPS, knowing
the location of the satellites based upon

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:09.650
their extremely predictable orbits, can figure
out where it is.

00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:11.740
So that’s how time developed.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:16.699
Hopefully you now see how the human conception
of time was essential for the development

00:06:16.699 --> 00:06:44.969
of our modern world and can still be called
one of humanity’s greatest inventions.

